  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 1223 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nCurrent upper air analysis depicts upper ridge axis over the \nCentral and Northern Plains. Skies over the CWA are mostly clear \nsave for a few upper level clouds. Sfc stations are reporting \nsoutherly winds with temperatures in the upper 60s to upper 70s \nacross much of the western SD plains. Slightly cooler temps (mid \n60s) are present over northeastern WY and the Black Hills. Way off\nto our west, deepening upper trof/closed low has pushed on shore \nover CA. Temperatures will continue to climb this afternoon with \nmany areas on the western SD plains hitting the 80 degree mark.\n\nMuch warmer temperatures expected Friday as WAA ramps up ahead of\napproaching upper trof and attendant cold front. Some spots on \nthe western SD plains could approach 90. Downtown Rapid City and \nthe airport will reach into the low to mid 80s, potentially \nbreaking their daily record highs (the daily record high for \ndowntown Rapid City for 10/23 was 82 set in 1952 and the record at\nthe airport was 80 set in 1963). In addition to the above average\ntemperatures, a tightening sfc pressure gradient courtesy of a \ndeveloping low will support gusty southwest winds over \nnortheastern WY. Winds over northeastern WY will begin to ramp up \nby mid-morning with sustained winds of 25 to 35 mph and gusts up \nto 50 mph. HDWI values are above the 95th percentile for Campbell \ninto Weston and Crook Counties. All this in mind, we are expecting\nwidespread critical fire wx conditions to develop by Friday \nafternoon. More details in our Fire Wx Discussion.\n\nStrong cold front pushes through the forecast area late Friday \nevening into early Saturday morning. Strong 3-hr pressure rises of\n4- 7 mb behind the front will support a brief period of strong \nnorthwesterly winds late Friday night/early Saturday morning as \nthe front pushes through. These pressure rises combined with \nstrong CAA and 850-700 mb winds of 30-50KT will support gusts of \n45 to 55 mph across much of the western SD plains. Will continue \nto monitor for potential wind headlines overnight Friday into \nearly Saturday. While some showers may accompany the front as it \ncomes through, not expecting any significant precip overnight \nFriday into Saturday.\n\nSaturday will be significantly and noticeably cooler with highs \nin the mid 40s to mid 50s. Winds will remain quite breezy over \nwestern SD through Saturday morning and early afternoon as the \nfront exits the region. Upper level low over the desert SW will \neject over the Central Plains by Sunday. Model guidance has \ncontinued to be in fairly good consensus of a band of light precip\n(rain and some snow) moving through the CWA Sunday afternoon into\nSunday evening. Deterministic guidance is hinting at the \npotential for some light accumulations (perhaps an inch or two), \nespecially over the higher elevations of the Black Hills and \nportions of northeastern WY. Though with warm ground temps and the\ntiming of the precip (looking like the bulk of the precip will \npush through in the afternoon hours), have low confidence in any \nappreciable snow accumulation except for maybe in the highest \nelevations of the Black Hills.\n\nSeasonable temps will continue through early next week as upper \ntrof moves through the plains with quasi-zonal flow developing by \nthe midweek. Next shot for precip looks to be in the Wed/Thu \ntimeframe.",
